        "## 5. Self-Consistency\n",
        "💡 Use Case: Run multiple variations of a prompt and select the most common response.\n",
        "\n",
        "🛠 Example: Determining the Most Reliable Answer\n",
        "\n",
        "**How Self-Consistency Works:**\n",
        "\n",
        "The idea behind self-consistency is to run the same prompt multiple times with slight variations (or even without variations) and then select the most frequent response. This technique leverages the idea that if the model produces the same answer multiple times, it's more likely to be the correct answer. It helps to mitigate the occasional inconsistencies or errors that LLMs might produce.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why It's Useful:**\n",
        "\n",
        "Increased Reliability: By selecting the most common response, we increase the likelihood of getting a reliable and accurate answer.\n",
        "Reduces Noise: It helps to filter out occasional incorrect or unexpected outputs from the LLM.\n",
        "Useful for Ambiguous Tasks: Self-consistency is particularly helpful for tasks where the model might have multiple valid interpretations or where the correct answer is not immediately obvious.\n",
        "\n",
        "To see the output, run the code."
